  "articleBody": "A garage door that sticks, jerks, or refuses to close completely is often suffering from a shifting frame rather than a motor defect. Ground settling, vehicle bumps, and untreated wood decay can pull vertical door jambs out of square, warping the steel tracks and putting extreme strain on rollers.\n\nMistakes to Avoid:\nThe most dangerous mistake is attempting to force the door closed using the opener limit screws. Forcing the motor against misaligned tracks can strip opener drive gears, bend panel hinges, or derail counterbalance cables. Another frequent error is replacing track rollers or adjusting spring tension while ignoring a rotten jamb base that flexes whenever the door moves.\n\nHow to Compare Your Options:\nDetermine whether the movement is isolated to an out-of-plumb vertical track or stems from rot in the framing studs. Track realignment is a fast fix when framing remains sound. If the jamb has detached from the concrete or the header is sagging, complete frame reconstruction is required to establish a square, stable opening.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps:\nCorrecting frame alignment often involves relieving track pressure while maintaining safety around heavy counterbalance springs. A skilled finish and framing contractor can rebuild the rough opening, square the casing, and ensure your tracks sit parallel.\n\nWhat to Do Next:\nIf your garage door is scraping or binding against the frame in Aurora or Centennial, call HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 for an on-site frame inspection.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
}